+## Engine
+
+The browser entry drives Bootstrap's markup on the platform's own mechanisms; its TypeScript
+follows Veneer's design rather than Bootstrap's JavaScript. Bootstrap's class tokens, `data-bs-*`
+attribute names, selectors, and wire event names are the engine's defaults, and each one is a
+constant in the browser entry, such as `BUTTON_ACTIVE`, `BUTTON_SELECTOR`, `TARGET_ATTRIBUTE`,
+`OPTION_PREFIX`, and `BUTTON_EVENTS`. Importing the entry installs no listener: you construct each
+engine yourself, or you construct a `Delegate` instance and it acquires engines from the data
+attributes. A TypeScript member keeps Veneer's naming, and where it departs from Bootstrap's name
+the departure is stated with the member: `destroy` replaces `dispose`, and the static `find` method
+replaces `getInstance`.
+
+The `resolveOptions` function merges an engine's options. The engine's defaults come first, then the
+keys it declares inside the host's `data-bs-config` JSON, then its declared `data-bs-*` attributes,
+then the object you pass to the constructor, each overriding the one before, and an explicit
+`false` or `0` survives every layer. A declared key is a one-word option key, which is Bootstrap's
+key verbatim, and its attribute is the `OPTION_PREFIX` constant followed by the key. An undeclared
+key is ignored, a `data-bs-config` value that is not a JSON record is ignored as Bootstrap ignores
+it, and a declared value that fails coercion throws an `AppError` carrying the engine's
+`{ENTITY}_OPTION_INVALID` code, the attribute's name, and the host's tag. Each declared key is
+coerced by the parser the engine declares for it rather than by Bootstrap's `normalizeData`
+function, so a key a boolean parser reads also accepts `1` and `0`. The `Button` class declares no
+key, as Bootstrap's Button declares no option, and reads its options from the constructor alone.
+
+### Events
+
+An engine dispatches its wire events on its host as bubbling `CustomEvent` instances through the
+`emitEvent` function. The engine passes the cancelable flag, and the function returns `false` when a
+listener's `preventDefault` call prevented a cancelable event, which is how an engine refuses a
+change a listener prevented; a listener's return value refuses nothing. Each field of a plain-record
+detail, such as Bootstrap's `relatedTarget`, travels in the event's `detail` and is also defined as
+an own property of the event, so a listener reads `event.relatedTarget` as it reads it under
+Bootstrap. The `toggle.vn.button` event has no Bootstrap counterpart, so it keeps its Veneer name
+and is not cancelable.
+
+An engine's wire table, such as `BUTTON_EVENTS`, maps each key of its event map to the wire name its
+event carries. The `on` option takes hooks keyed the same way, and the `bindEventMap` function binds
+them to the wire names until the engine is destroyed. An event under a bound name whose payload
+fails the engine's event guard, such as the `isButtonEvent` function, reaches no hook.
+
+### Delegation
+
+The `Delegate` class is the engine's only data API. It listens for clicks on its root, the document
+unless you pass another, and reads each route from a constant: a click inside a host the
+`BUTTON_SELECTOR` constant matches drives that host's button. That is the engine the static `find`
+method returns, whoever constructed it, or an engine the delegate constructs and acquires when the
+host has none. The delegate prevents the click's default action, as Bootstrap's data API does. A
+host under nested delegate roots is driven once per click, by whichever delegate hears the click
+first.
+
+While the delegate owns an engine it observes its root, and at the observer delivery that follows a
+removal it restores and releases every host it acquired that the root no longer contains, whether
+the host left the document or moved outside the root. A host removed and reinserted within one task
+keeps its engine and its state, because the root contains it again when the delivery arrives. A
+host reinserted after its release is acquired again, with a fresh engine, on its next delegated
+click. Destruction removes the listener and the observer and restores every host the delegate still
+owns; an engine you constructed stays yours.
+
+A trigger names the element it controls by the attribute the `TARGET_ATTRIBUTE` constant names, or
+by the fragment of the attribute the `LINK_ATTRIBUTE` constant names when the target attribute is
+absent, empty, or a bare `#`. The `readTarget` and `readTargets` functions resolve that selector as
+Bootstrap's `getSelector` function does, escaping each id through `CSS.escape`, and return only
+HTML elements.
+
+### Ownership and restoration
+
+Each engine class keeps one `Registry` instance, so a host carries at most one engine of each class.
+Construction validates the host, then claims it before binding any hook, and a second engine of the
+same class on the same host throws an `AppError` carrying the class's `{ENTITY}_HOST_OWNED` code,
+such as `BUTTON_HOST_OWNED`. The static `find` method returns the live engine that owns a host.
+Destruction releases the claim, so the host can take a fresh engine afterwards.
+
+Each engine keeps one `Snapshot` instance. It records every attribute, class token, and inline
+property the engine writes before the first write, and destruction writes each recorded value back:
+a value recorded as absent is removed, and a `class` attribute the engine's writes created is
+removed when no token remains in it. State the snapshot never recorded keeps every edit you made.
+
+### Motion
+
+The `settleAnimations` function waits for an element's transitions. It reads the element's own
+animations, leaves infinite and paused animations out, and resolves when each running animation
+finishes, is cancelled, or leaves with its element. It re-reads the list after each round, so a
+transition that starts when another finishes is awaited as well. No timer and no emulated
+`transitionend` event stand in for the animation. A transition of zero length creates no animation,
+so under reduced motion the wait resolves in a microtask. Aborting the signal the function takes
+resolves the wait at once.
+
+A class or dimension written on an element whose style the browser has not computed starts no
+transition. The `reflow` function forces that computation, so the write that follows it in the same
+task transitions from the element's present state.
+
